  <!-- Q1: CHARGE Syndrome -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 1</span>
      <span>Category: Pediatric Congenital Anomalies</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A newborn male presents with respiratory distress that resolves when he cries vigorously but worsens during feeding. Attempts to pass a small nasogastric catheter through either naris meet firm resistance in the posterior nasal cavity. Physical examination reveals bilateral iris colobomas, low-set cup-shaped ears with sensorineural deafness, micropenis, and a harsh holosystolic murmur secondary to a ventricular septal defect.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following genetic syndromes is most strongly associated with this constellation of congenital findings?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome)</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> CHARGE syndrome (<em>CHD7</em> gene mutation)</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Pierre Robin sequence with mandibular hypoplasia</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis)</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> VACTERL association with vertebral abnormalities</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The patient exhibits <strong>CHARGE syndrome</strong>, an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the <em>CHD7</em> gene. The classic mnemonic includes: <strong>C</strong>oloboma (most commonly chorioretinal or iris), <strong>H</strong>eart defects (e.g., VSD, tetralogy of Fallot), <strong>A</strong>tresia of the choanae (bilateral bony/membranous obstruction causing obligate mouth breathing), <strong>R</strong>etardation of growth and development, <strong>G</strong>enital hypoplasia, and <strong>E</strong>ar abnormalities/deafness. Over 50% of bilateral choanal atresia cases occur in the setting of CHARGE syndrome.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> DiGeorge syndrome presents with thymic hypoplasia, hypocalcemia, and conotruncal cardiac defects, but not classic bilateral choanal atresia.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> Pierre Robin sequence features micrognathia, glossoptosis, and cleft palate, without choanal atresia or iris colobomas.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> Treacher Collins features downward-slanting palpebral fissures and zygomatic hypoplasia without colobomas or choanal atresia.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> VACTERL includes vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal fistula, renal, and limb anomalies, not choanal atresia or colobomas.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Congenital Anomalies of the Respiratory System.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 43</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q2: CPAM Type 2 -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 2</span>
      <span>Category: Congenital Lung Malformations</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A newborn female undergoes evaluation for tachypnea and mild intercostal retractions. Chest CT demonstrates a multicystic lesion in the right lower lobe comprising numerous, uniform, small cysts measuring approximately 0.5 to 1.5 cm in diameter. Renal ultrasound reveals concomitant bilateral renal agenesis (Potter sequence phenotype). Histopathological examination of the resected lobe reveals cyst walls lined by simple ciliated cuboidal-to-columnar epithelium resembling terminal bronchioles, devoid of cartilage or mucous cells.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      According to the Stocker classification of Congenital Pulmonary Airway Malformations (CPAM), which of the following types is present in this infant?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Type 0 CPAM (Acinar dysgenesis)</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Type 1 CPAM (Large dominant cysts)</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Type 2 CPAM (Multiple uniform small cysts)</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Type 3 CPAM (Microcystic / solid adenomatoid)</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Type 4 CPAM (Distal alveolar peripheral cysts)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        <strong>CPAM Type 2</strong> accounts for 15% to 20% of cases and consists of <strong>multiple, uniform small cysts (typically &lt;2 cm in diameter)</strong> lined by bronchiolar-type epithelium. Notably, Type 2 CPAM is notorious for its strong association with <strong>other congenital anomalies</strong> (in over 50% of cases), particularly renal anomalies (renal agenesis/dysgenesis), cardiovascular defects, and skeletal malformations.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Type 0 is rare acinar dysgenesis affecting the entire lung, incompatible with life.</li>
          <li><em>Option B:</em> Type 1 is the most common (60–70%) and features large single or multiple macroscopic cysts (>2 cm, up to 10 cm) with good prognosis.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> Type 3 is bulky, microcystic or solid (<0.2 cm) adenomatoid lesion affecting whole lobes.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Type 4 features large peripheral thin-walled cysts derived from alveolar saccular tissue, prone to pleuropulmonary blastoma.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Congenital Anomalies of the Respiratory System.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 37</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q3: Laryngeal Web -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 3</span>
      <span>Category: Pediatric Airway Embryology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A full-term male newborn is noted to have a hoarse, weak, barely audible cry since birth. Over the subsequent 48 hours, he exhibits mild biphasic stridor that becomes prominent when agitated. Direct flexible laryngoscopy reveals a translucent, web-like fibrous sheet connecting the vocal cords anteriorly across the glottic aperture, occluding approximately 40% of the anterior laryngeal lumen.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following embryological mechanisms is responsible for the formation of this congenital laryngeal web?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Incomplete recanalization of the primitive laryngeal lumen during the 10th week</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Failure of the neural crest mesenchyme of the 4th arch to migrate into the arytenoid swellings</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Premature regression of the second pharyngeal arch cartilage (Reichert's cartilage)</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Defective lateral fusion of the right and left medial nasal prominences</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Aberrant longitudinal development of the tracheoesophageal septum</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: A</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        During normal laryngeal morphogenesis, rapid endodermal epithelial proliferation temporarily obliterates the laryngeal lumen. In the <strong>10th week of gestation</strong>, this epithelial lamina undergoes <strong>recanalization</strong> via programmed apoptosis, forming the laryngeal ventricles and vocal folds. <strong>Incomplete recanalization</strong> leaves a persistent tissue bridge, usually at the level of the true vocal cords anteriorly, resulting in a <strong>congenital laryngeal web</strong> manifested by a hoarse cry and stridor.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option B:</em> Defective 4th/6th arch neural crest migration results in severe cartilage agenesis or tracheobronchomalacia.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> The 2nd arch forms the stapes, styloid process, and lesser horn of the hyoid, having no role in glottic recanalization.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> Medial nasal prominence fusion forms the intermaxillary segment (philtrum and primary palate).</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Tracheoesophageal septum defects result in tracheoesophageal fistulae.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Development of the Respiratory System.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 41</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q4: Flail Chest -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 4</span>
      <span>Category: Chest Wall Trauma & Mechanics</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 35-year-old male is brought to the trauma center following a high-speed motor vehicle collision where his chest impacted the steering wheel. He exhibits severe dyspnea, tachypnea (34/min), and localized chest wall contusions. During spontaneous breathing, a segment of the left anterior chest wall moves inward during inspiration and bulges outward during expiration.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following pathophysiological descriptions accurately defines this mechanical breathing abnormality?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Increased outward compliance of the thoracic cage due to isolated sternal fracture</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Paradoxical chest wall motion due to segmental fractures of at least 3 consecutive ribs in 2 places</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Asymmetric diaphragmatic excursion resulting from complete unilateral phrenic nerve transection</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Dynamic airway collapse caused by rupture of the membranous posterior trachealis muscle</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Mediastinal fluttering triggered by unilateral open sucking pneumothorax</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The patient has a <strong>flail chest</strong>, defined by the fracture of <strong>&ge;3 contiguous ribs in &ge;2 separate locations</strong> (or rib fractures combined with sternal separation). This detaches an isolated segment of the thoracic cage from the remainder of the chest wall. During inspiration, the generation of negative intrathoracic pressure sucks the flail segment inward; during expiration, positive pleural pressure pushes the unstable segment outward. This <strong>paradoxical chest wall motion</strong> significantly increases the work of breathing and is typically complicated by underlying pulmonary contusion.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Isolated single fractures do not create a free-floating segment capable of paradoxical breathing.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> Phrenic nerve palsy causes elevated hemidiaphragm with paradoxical upward diaphragmatic movement, not chest wall flail.</li>
          <li><em>Option D & E:</em> Trachealis rupture or open pneumothorax causes air leaks and mediastinal shift, but not localized multi-segmental paradoxical chest wall instability.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: mechanics of respiration.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 49</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q5: Open Pneumothorax Intrapleural Pressure -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 5</span>
      <span>Category: Respiratory Mechanics & Pressures</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 26-year-old male sustains a penetrating knife wound to the right lateral chest wall, creating a 4 cm gaping defect in the thoracic cage. Air is heard audibly sucking in and out through the wound with each respiratory effort. Arterial blood gas reveals severe hypoxemia and respiratory acidosis.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Under these pathological conditions, which of the following values best characterizes the static intrapleural pressure within the affected right hemithorax?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Maintained at normal resting subatmospheric levels (-4 to -6 cmH2O)</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Equilibrates with atmospheric pressure (zero cmH2O transpulmonary pressure gradient)</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Exceeds atmospheric pressure by more than +20 cmH2O throughout inspiration</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Fluctuates between -15 cmH2O on inspiration and -8 cmH2O on expiration</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Becomes strongly negative due to unopposed diaphragmatic contraction</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        In an <strong>open ("sucking") pneumothorax</strong>, a defect in the chest wall directly communicates the intrapleural space with the external environment. As atmospheric air rushes in through the path of least resistance, the normally negative (subatmospheric) intrapleural pressure is lost and <strong>equilibrates with atmospheric pressure (intrapleural pressure becomes zero)</strong>. Without a negative intrapleural pressure, transpulmonary pressure (P_alveolar - P_intrapleural) drops to zero, and the natural elastic recoil of the lung causes immediate complete ipsilateral lung collapse.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Subatmospheric pressure is abolished as atmospheric air enters the pleural space.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> Persistent pressures >+20 cmH2O characterize a <em>tension pneumothorax</em> with a one-way ball-valve mechanism.</li>
          <li><em>Option D & E:</em> Strongly negative pressures occur during vigorous inspiration against an occluded airway (Müller maneuver), not an open chest wall defect.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: mechanics of respiration.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 24</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q6: Diffusion-Limited Gas Exchange in Strenuous Exercise -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 6</span>
      <span>Category: Gas Exchange Physiology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 24-year-old elite triathlete performs maximal exercise testing on a cycle ergometer at high workload. At peak exertion, his cardiac output increases from a baseline of 5 L/min to 25 L/min. Pulmonary blood flow velocity increases substantially, reducing the transit time of erythrocytes through pulmonary capillaries from the resting 0.75 seconds down to approximately 0.22 seconds.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Under these conditions of extreme physiological stress or in the presence of alveolar-capillary thickening, which of the following describes the dynamics of pulmonary oxygen transfer?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Oxygen transfer remains strictly perfusion-limited because diffusion capacity increases linearly</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Oxygen transfer shifts from perfusion-limited to diffusion-limited due to incomplete equilibration</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Carbon dioxide transfer becomes diffusion-limited before oxygen due to lower solubility</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient narrows to zero as capillary recruitment peaks</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Pulmonary capillary transit time exceeds the minimum required equilibration threshold</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Under normal resting conditions, oxygen is <strong>perfusion-limited</strong>: capillary blood PO2 completely equilibrates with alveolar PO2 within the first 0.25 seconds (one-third) of the 0.75-second capillary transit time. During <strong>strenuous exercise</strong> (or in diseases with alveolar membrane thickening like pulmonary fibrosis), transit time decreases dramatically (<0.25 seconds). Blood exits the capillary before full alveolar-capillary PO2 equilibration can occur. Thus, gas exchange transitions to become <strong>diffusion-limited</strong>, resulting in exercise-induced hypoxemia and an widened A-a gradient.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Oxygen transfer does not remain perfusion-limited when transit time falls below the equilibration threshold.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> CO2 has a diffusing capacity ~20 times higher than O2 due to its high solubility, making O2 diffusion-limited long before CO2.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> The A-a gradient widens (increases), rather than narrowing to zero.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Transit time drops below (not exceeds) the threshold necessary for complete equilibration.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Pulmonary circulation and gas exchange -II.pdf &amp; TBL Year 3 Recall Item 8</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q7: Dead Space and Alveolar Ventilation -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 7</span>
      <span>Category: Pulmonary Function & Ventilation</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      Two medical students participate in a pulmonary physiology lab. Both subjects maintain an identical minute ventilation of 6,000 mL/min. Subject 1 breathes shallowly and rapidly at a tidal volume of 250 mL and a respiratory rate of 24 breaths/min. Subject 2 breathes deeply and slowly at a tidal volume of 600 mL and a respiratory rate of 10 breaths/min. Both have an anatomical dead space of 150 mL.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following physiological principles best explains why Subject 2 achieves significantly higher effective alveolar ventilation than Subject 1?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Slower breathing rates cause a physical decrease in anatomical dead space volume</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Larger tidal volumes minimize the fraction of each breath wasted in physiological dead space</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Rapid shallow breathing increases the diffusing capacity of the alveolar-capillary membrane</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> High respiratory rates stimulate pulmonary surfactant secretion via vagal mechanoreceptors</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Slower breathing rates completely eliminate turbulent airflow in conducting airways</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Alveolar ventilation is calculated as: $V_A = (V_T - V_D) \times RR$. For Subject 1: $(250 - 150) \times 24 = 2,400\text{ mL/min}$. For Subject 2: $(600 - 150) \times 10 = 4,500\text{ mL/min}$. Although total minute ventilation ($V_E = V_T \times RR$) is identical (6,000 mL/min), Subject 2 achieves almost double the effective alveolar ventilation because a larger tidal volume dilutes dead space, **decreasing the proportion of ventilation wasted in physiological dead space** and reducing the gap between minute ventilation and alveolar ventilation.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Anatomical dead space is a fixed physical volume (~150 mL) determined by conducting airway geometry, not breathing frequency.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> Rapid shallow breathing does not increase membrane diffusing capacity.</li>
          <li><em>Option D & E:</em> Surfactant and turbulent flow dynamics do not explain the quantitative difference in dead space fraction.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Pulmonary function tests in respiratory disorders.pdf &amp; TBL Year 3 Recall Item 1</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q8: SAMA (Ipratropium) in COPD with Arrhythmia -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 8</span>
      <span>Category: Respiratory Pharmacology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 72-year-old male with severe COPD and a history of ischemic heart disease with frequent paroxysmal atrial fibrillation presents with acute worsening of breathlessness. His resting heart rate is 108/min. The clinical team requires an inhaled bronchodilator that will provide effective relief of bronchoconstriction while minimizing the risk of worsening sinus tachycardia or precipitating supraventricular tachyarrhythmias.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following inhaled bronchodilator agents has the lowest potential to cause cardiovascular arrhythmias in this patient?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Inhaled salbutamol (albuterol)</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Inhaled formoterol</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Inhaled ipratropium bromide</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Intravenous aminophylline</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Subcutaneous terbutaline</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        <strong>Ipratropium bromide</strong> is a quaternary ammonium Short-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (SAMA). Because of its permanent positive charge, it is minimally absorbed across mucosal surfaces into the systemic circulation. By acting locally to block M3 muscarinic receptors in bronchial smooth muscle, it produces bronchodilation with **virtually no systemic antimuscarinic or chronotropic cardiac side effects**. In contrast, beta2-agonists (salbutamol, formoterol) and methylxanthines directly stimulate cardiac beta1/beta2 receptors, carrying significant risks of tachycardia and arrhythmias.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A, B, & E:</em> Beta2-agonists stimulate cardiac receptors and cause hypokalemia, both of which trigger tachyarrhythmias.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> Aminophylline inhibits phosphodiesterase and blocks adenosine receptors, possessing a narrow therapeutic index with high proarrhythmic potential.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Treatment of Bronchial Asthma.pptx &amp; TBL Year 3 Recall Item 2</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q9: H1 Antihistamines Ineffective in Asthma -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 9</span>
      <span>Category: Asthma Pharmacology & Immunology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 16-year-old female with moderate persistent allergic asthma and seasonal allergic rhinitis asks her physician whether taking over-the-counter second-generation H1-receptor antihistamines (e.g., cetirizine) will improve her daily asthma control and reduce her frequency of wheezing exacerbations.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following immunological and pharmacological principles explains why H1 antihistamines are ineffective in the clinical management of bronchial asthma?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Airway mast cells do not synthesize or store preformed histamine granules</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Bronchoconstriction in asthma is mediated primarily by leukotrienes and other mediators rather than histamine</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Histamine H1 receptors are entirely absent from human bronchial smooth muscle membranes</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Second-generation antihistamines undergo rapid local enzymatic cleavage by airway epithelial peptidases</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Antihistamines paradoxically trigger the recruitment of eosinophils via major basic protein induction</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Although histamine is released from mast cells during early acute allergen exposure, it plays only a minor, transient role in bronchospasm. The sustained bronchoconstriction, airway hyperresponsiveness, mucus hypersecretion, and tissue remodeling of asthma are driven primarily by **cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4)—which are 1,000 times more potent than histamine**—along with PGD2, PAF, and Th2 cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13). Consequently, selective **blockade of H1 receptors is ineffective** for asthma maintenance.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Airway mast cells contain abundant histamine stored in preformed granules.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> H1 receptors are present on bronchial smooth muscle, but their activation contributes little to chronic asthma symptoms compared to leukotrienes.</li>
          <li><em>Option D & E:</em> Antihistamines are not broken down by airway peptidases nor do they induce eosinophilia.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Immunopathology of asthma- L1.pdf &amp; TBL Year 3 Recall Item 6</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q10: Th2-High vs. Th2-Low Asthma (Neutrophilia) -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 10</span>
      <span>Category: Asthma Endotypes & Immunology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 52-year-old female with severe adult-onset asthma refractory to high-dose inhaled corticosteroids undergoes endotypic phenotyping. Sputum cytology reveals 68% neutrophils and &lt;1% eosinophils. Serum total IgE is 18 IU/mL (normal &lt;100 IU/mL), and fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO) is 12 ppb (normal &lt;25 ppb). Genetic testing shows no upregulation of IL-4 or IL-13 receptors.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following inflammatory features is characteristically associated with **Type 2-low (non-T2)** asthma rather than Type 2-high asthma?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Prominent eosinophilic airway infiltration mediated by interleukin-5</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Elevated fractional exhaled nitric oxide driven by interleukin-13</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> High circulating serum IgE antibodies directed against aeroallergens</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Neutrophilic airway inflammation mediated by Th17 cells and interleukin-8</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Excellent clinical responsiveness to anti-IL-4/13 monoclonal antibodies</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: D</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Asthma is classified into **Type 2-high (T2-high)** and **Type 2-low (non-T2)** endotypes. T2-high is driven by Th2 cells and ILC2s producing IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, leading to eosinophilia, elevated FeNO, high IgE, and excellent response to corticosteroids and biologics (omalizumab, mepolizumab, dupilumab). In contrast, **Type 2-low asthma is characterized by neutrophilic or paucigranulocytic inflammation**, driven by Th1/Th17 pathways and cytokines like **IL-8, IL-17, and TNF-alpha**, and is notoriously resistant to standard steroid therapy.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Options A, B, C, & E:</em> Eosinophils, elevated FeNO, high IgE, and responsiveness to dupilumab are classic hallmarks of T2-high asthma, not T2-low asthma.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Immunopathology of asthma- L1.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 17</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q11: Zileuton 5-Lipoxygenase Pathway -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 11</span>
      <span>Category: Respiratory Pharmacology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 28-year-old male with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD) presents with recurrent nasal polyps, severe asthma attacks following NSAID ingestion, and chronic rhinosinusitis. His physician prescribes an oral medication that directly blocks the biochemical conversion of arachidonic acid to 5-hydroperoxyeicosatetraenoic acid (5-HPETE).
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following pharmacological agents acts through this exact enzymatic inhibitory mechanism?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Montelukast</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Zafirlukast</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Zileuton</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Pranlukast</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Roflumilast</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Leukotriene modifiers are divided into two distinct mechanistic classes:
        1. **5-Lipoxygenase (5-LO) inhibitors:** **Zileuton** is the sole approved agent in this class; it directly inhibits the 5-LO enzyme, preventing the synthesis of all leukotrienes (LTB4, LTC4, LTD4, LTE4).
        2. **CysLT1 receptor antagonists:** Montelukast, zafirlukast, and pranlukast selectively block the CysLT1 receptor downstream, without inhibiting 5-LO.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A, B, & D:</em> Montelukast, zafirlukast, and pranlukast are CysLT1 receptor antagonists; they do not inhibit 5-lipoxygenase enzyme synthesis.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Roflumilast is a phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE-4) inhibitor used in severe COPD.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Treatment of Bronchial Asthma.pptx / Immunopathology of asthma- L1.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 5</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q12: Aspiration Pneumonia Microaerophilic / Anaerobic Pathogen -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 12</span>
      <span>Category: Infectious Disease Pathology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 58-year-old male with severe chronic alcohol use disorder is found unresponsive on the floor of his apartment. Four days later, he presents to the emergency department with high fever, foul-smelling putrid sputum, and right-sided pleuritic chest pain. Physical examination reveals poor dentition with heavy periodontal disease. Chest CT demonstrates a thick-walled cavitary lesion containing an air-fluid level located in the superior segment of the right lower lobe.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following bacterial genera is the most common anaerobic pathogen implicated in this patient's necrotizing lung abscess?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> <em>Pseudomonas</em></li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> <em>Bacteroides</em></li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> <em>Mycoplasma</em></li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> <em>Legionella</em></li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> <em>Streptococcus</em></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The patient has an **aspiration lung abscess**, classic in alcoholics and patients with altered consciousness or compromised gag reflexes. Microaspiration of oral flora from periodontal crevices seeds dependent segments (superior segment of lower lobes or posterior segment of upper lobes). The characteristic **putrid, foul-smelling sputum** and cavitation are pathognomonic for **oral anaerobes**, predominantly ***Bacteroides* species (e.g., *B. melaninogenicus / Prevotella* and *B. fragilis*)**, *Peptostreptococcus*, and *Fusobacterium nucleatum*.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> is an aerobic Gram-negative rod causing ventilator-associated pneumonia, not putrid anaerobic abscesses.</li>
          <li><em>Option C & D:</em> <em>Mycoplasma</em> and <em>Legionella</em> are atypical pathogens causing non-cavitary interstitial or lobar pneumonia.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> causes typical lobar consolidation with rust-colored sputum, rarely causing cavitary abscesses.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Respiratory Infections &amp; Clinical Reasoning Study Guide.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 34</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q13: Fusarium Mycology -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 13</span>
      <span>Category: Medical Mycology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 49-year-old female undergoing allogeneic stem cell transplantation for refractory leukemia develops severe neutropenia (ANC 50/mcL). On day 21, she develops persistent high fever, cough, and multiple tender, erythematous skin nodules with central necrotic ecthyma-gangrenosum-like eschars. Blood cultures are positive for a filamentous hyaline mold. Microscopic evaluation demonstrates branched hyphae with characteristic curved, multiseptate, sickle- or canoe-shaped macroconidia.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following opportunistic fungi is the causative agent of this disseminated hyalohyphomycosis?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> <em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em></li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> <em>Cryptococcus neoformans</em></li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> <em>Fusarium solani</em></li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> <em>Rhizopus arrhizus</em></li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> <em>Histoplasma capsulatum</em></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        ***Fusarium* species** are opportunistic hyaline molds (hyalohyphomycosis) that cause severe, often fatal disseminated disease in severely neutropenic hematology patients. Unlike *Aspergillus* (which rarely yields positive blood cultures), ***Fusarium* characteristically produces positive blood cultures in &gt;50% of disseminated cases** and metastatic necrotic targetoid cutaneous lesions. Microscopically, it is recognized by its pathognomonic **sickle-, boat-, or canoe-shaped, multicellular macroconidia**.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> <em>Aspergillus</em> produces dichotomous 45° acute branching hyphae with conidiophores bearing phialides; blood cultures are almost always negative.</li>
          <li><em>Option B:</em> <em>Cryptococcus</em> is an encapsulated monomorphic budding yeast, not a mold with macroconidia.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> <em>Rhizopus</em> (Mucorales) produces broad, non-septate, right-angle (90°) ribbon-like hyphae.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> <em>Histoplasma</em> is a dimorphic fungus exhibiting intracellular oval yeasts in macrophages.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Respiratory Infections &amp; Clinical Reasoning Study Guide.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 35</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q14: Internal Branch of Superior Laryngeal Nerve -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 14</span>
      <span>Category: Laryngeal Anatomy & Neurophysiology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 64-year-old female undergoes transoral surgical resection of a pyriform sinus tumor. During dissection along the lateral wall of the laryngeal vestibule, a nerve piercing the thyrohyoid membrane alongside the superior laryngeal artery is accidentally transected. Postoperatively, the patient's voice is unaffected and vocal cord abduction/adduction is fully preserved, but she repeatedly aspirates thin liquids and fails to cough when foreign particles enter the supraglottic laryngeal vestibule.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following nerves was transected during the procedure?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Recurrent laryngeal nerve</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Pharyngeal branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Hypoglossal nerve motor trunk</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The **internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve** (a branch of CN X) pierces the thyrohyoid membrane together with the superior laryngeal artery. It is purely sensory and carries general sensation from the laryngeal mucosa **above the vocal cords (the supraglottic region and vocal folds)**, serving as the essential afferent limb of the cough reflex to prevent aspiration. Transection abolishes the protective cough reflex without causing motor paralysis of the intrinsic laryngeal muscles.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> The recurrent laryngeal nerve provides sensory innervation <em>below</em> the vocal cords and motor innervation to all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid.</li>
          <li><em>Option B:</em> The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve provides motor innervation to the cricothyroid muscle (tenses vocal cords).</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> Glossopharyngeal branches innervate the oropharynx, base of tongue, and gag reflex afferent.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> The hypoglossal nerve is the motor nerve to intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Anatomy of the Upper respiratory Tract.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 23</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q15: Kiesselbach's Plexus & Anterior Ethmoidal Artery -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 15</span>
      <span>Category: Upper Airway Vascular Anatomy</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 14-year-old boy presents to the clinic with recurrent epistaxis from the anterior nasal septum. Anterior rhinoscopy identifies active bleeding from a dilated mucosal vascular network located in Little's area (Kiesselbach's plexus). The attending physician reviews the four anastomosing arterial sources supplying this region.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following arteries supplying this vascular plexus is a direct branch of the **internal carotid artery** system?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Sphenopalatine artery</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Greater palatine artery</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Superior labial artery (septal branch)</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Anterior ethmoidal artery</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Ascending pharyngeal artery</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: D</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Kiesselbach's plexus (Little's area) on the anteroinferior nasal septum is the site of >90% of epistaxis episodes. It is formed by an anastomosis of four arteries:
        1. **Anterior ethmoidal artery** (from the ophthalmic artery, which is a branch of the **Internal Carotid Artery**).
        2. Sphenopalatine artery (from the maxillary artery, external carotid).
        3. Greater palatine artery (from the maxillary artery, external carotid).
        4. Superior labial artery (from the facial artery, external carotid).
        The anterior ethmoidal artery is the unique participant supplied via the internal carotid artery.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Options A, B, & C:</em> The sphenopalatine, greater palatine, and superior labial arteries are all derived from the <em>External Carotid Artery</em> system.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> The ascending pharyngeal artery is an external carotid branch that does not contribute to Kiesselbach's plexus.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Anatomy of the Upper respiratory Tract.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 47</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q16: Flail Chest / AFE Exclusion -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 16</span>
      <span>Category: Obstetric Respiratory Emergencies</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 31-year-old G2P1 at 39 weeks of gestation undergoes acute induction of labor. Shortly after artificial rupture of membranes, she experiences sudden, catastrophic breathlessness, cyanosis, profound hypotension (BP 60/40 mmHg), and generalized tonic-clonic seizures, followed by profuse hemorrhage from her episiotomy wound and IV insertion sites. The clinical team immediately suspects Amniotic Fluid Embolism (AFE).
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following clinical or laboratory features is **NOT** a recognized pathological manifestation of Amniotic Fluid Embolism?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Sudden acute hypoxemic respiratory failure and pulmonary edema</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Severe cardiogenic shock and hemodynamic collapse</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Disseminated intravascular coagulation with consumptive hemorrhage</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Secondary maternal absolute polycythemia (erythrocytosis)</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Neurological manifestations including seizures and coma</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: D</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        **Amniotic Fluid Embolism (AFE)** is an acute, fulminant peripartum catastrophe characterized by the classic tetrad of:
        1. Acute respiratory distress and profound hypoxemia.
        2. Hemodynamic collapse and cardiogenic shock.
        3. Consumptive coagulopathy / DIC with massive hemorrhage.
        4. Neurological insult (seizures, altered mental status, coma).
        Because AFE is an acute, catastrophic event occurring over minutes to hours, **maternal polycythemia (erythrocytosis) does NOT occur** (polycythemia requires weeks of chronic hypoxia to stimulate renal erythropoietin).
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Options A, B, C, & E:</em> Acute hypoxemic failure, cardiogenic shock, DIC with consumptive bleeding, and seizures are hallmark recognized manifestations of AFE.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Respiratory_Diseases_in_Pregnancy_Year3_November 2026.pdf — Slide: "Amniotic Fluid Embolism" &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 15</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q17: Renal Longest Buffer System -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 17</span>
      <span>Category: Acid-Base Physiology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A clinical physiologist presents a lecture on systemic acid-base buffering mechanisms following metabolic or respiratory disturbances. He contrasts the rapid chemical buffering by extracellular bicarbonate, intracellular hemoglobin/proteins, and the immediate ventilatory response of the respiratory center with the slower organ-based regulatory mechanisms.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following organ systems provides the most powerful, complete, but slowest-acting physiological mechanism for long-term compensation of acid-base disorders?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Pulmonary alveolar ventilation via carbon dioxide elimination</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Hepatic ureagenesis and ammonium transamination</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Renal regulation via hydrogen excretion and bicarbonate reabsorption</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Skeletal bone mineralization via calcium phosphate dissolution</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Gastrointestinal secretion of hydrochloric acid into gastric juice</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The body maintains acid-base balance across three progressive lines of defense:
        1. **Chemical buffers (bicarbonate, phosphate, proteins):** Act in fractions of a second.
        2. **Respiratory center (CO2 blow-off or retention):** Acts within minutes to hours.
        3. **Renal mechanism:** The **slowest but most definitive and powerful buffer system**. It requires **2 to 5 days** to achieve maximal compensation by modulating H+ excretion, reabsorbing filtered HCO3-, and generating new bicarbonate via ammoniagenesis ($NH_4^+$) and titratable acid ($H_2PO_4^-$).
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> The respiratory system acts rapidly (minutes to hours), but cannot fully restore pH to normal and cannot eliminate fixed metabolic acids.</li>
          <li><em>Option B, D, & E:</em> The liver, bones, and GI tract contribute to buffering, but the kidneys represent the ultimate physiological regulatory system for bicarbonate homeostasis.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: ABG acid-base balance.pdf &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 22</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q18: Ambroxol Mucolytic Mechanism -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 18</span>
      <span>Category: Pharmacology of Cough & Secretions</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 50-year-old male with chronic bronchitis presents with persistent productive cough and complaints of thick, tenacious, stringy sputum that is extremely difficult to expectorate. His physician prescribes oral ambroxol tablets as adjunctive mucolytic therapy.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following biochemical actions describes the primary mechanism by which ambroxol reduces sputum viscosity?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Depolymerization and breakdown of acidic mucopolysaccharide fibers in bronchial secretions</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Irreversible covalent cleavage of disulfide bridges between mucin monomers via free sulfhydryl groups</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Direct enzymatic degradation of extracellular DNA polymers derived from autolyzed neutrophils</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Osmotic extraction of water from the submucosa via selective activation of apical CFTR channels</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Competitive inhibition of neutral endopeptidase preventing breakdown of substance P</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: A</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        **Ambroxol** (the active metabolite of bromhexine) is a mucolytic agent that acts by **depolymerizing and breaking down acidic mucopolysaccharide fibers** within bronchial secretions. Additionally, ambroxol stimulates alveolar type II pneumocytes to synthesize and secrete pulmonary surfactant, which reduces the adhesiveness of secretions against the mucosal surface, facilitating mucociliary transport and expectoration.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option B:</em> Cleavage of disulfide bonds via free sulfhydryl groups is the mechanism of N-acetylcysteine (NAC).</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> Enzymatic degradation of extracellular neutrophil DNA is the mechanism of recombinant human dornase alfa (DNase).</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> Inhaled hypertonic saline draws water osmotically, not ambroxol.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Neutral endopeptidase inhibition is related to neprilysin inhibitors, not mucolytics.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Treatment of Cough.pptx &amp; Final Exam Recall Item 29</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q19: Butamirate -->
