      <span>Question 20</span>
      <span>Category: Thoracic Topographical Anatomy</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A medical student performs physical examination on a patient suspected of having right middle lobe pneumonia. To delineate the surface anatomical boundaries of the right middle lobe, she must correctly locate the horizontal fissure on the anterior and lateral thoracic wall.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following thoracic surface landmarks accurately corresponds to the anatomical course of the **horizontal fissure of the right lung**?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Begins at the spine of the T3 vertebra posteriorly and descends obliquely to the 6th costochondral junction</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Begins at the 4th costal cartilage anteriorly and extends horizontally laterally to meet the oblique fissure at the 5th rib in the midaxillary line</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Follows the inferior border of the 2nd rib horizontally from the sternal angle to the midclavicular line</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Extends vertically from the 1st costal cartilage downward along the lateral sternal border to the 7th costal margin</li>
