      <li><strong>B.</strong> Lower six intercostal and subcostal nerves (T7–T12)</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Vagus nerve (CN X sensory fibers)</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (L1)</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Greater splanchnic sympathetic nerves (T5–T9)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The diaphragm has dual sensory innervation:
        1. **Central tendon and mediastinal/central diaphragmatic pleura/peritoneum:** Innervated by the **phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5)**; pain here is referred to the **ipsilateral shoulder tip / supraclavicular region (C4 dermatome)**.
        2. **Peripheral muscular rim and costal diaphragmatic pleura/peritoneum:** Innervated by the **lower 6 intercostal and subcostal nerves (T7–T12)**; pain here is referred locally to the **thoracic cage and anterolateral abdominal wall (T7–T12 dermatomes)**.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> The phrenic nerve supplies motor innervation to the entire diaphragm and sensory innervation to the <em>central tendon</em> (referring pain to the shoulder tip).</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> The vagus nerve does not provide somatosensory innervation to the diaphragmatic parietal pleura.</li>
          <li><em>Option D & E:</em> Iliohypogastric/ilioinguinal nerves and splanchnic nerves do not innervate the diaphragm.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Anatomy of the lower respiratory tract.pdf &amp; Theory Screenshot Recall Item 16</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q22: Trachealis Muscle Histology & Innervation -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 22</span>
      <span>Category: Respiratory Histology & Autonomic Control</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A pathologist examines a cross-section of the human cervical trachea. She identifies 16 to 20 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings that maintain patency of the anterior and lateral walls. The posterior wall is devoid of cartilage and bridged by a fibroelastic membrane containing a band of transverse smooth muscle fibers directly abutting the anterior esophageal wall.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      What is the anatomical name of this posterior smooth muscle band, and which autonomic pathway stimulates its contraction during the explosive phase of coughing?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Vocalis muscle, stimulated by postganglionic sympathetic cervical fibers</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Cricopharyngeus muscle, stimulated by somatic recurrent laryngeal motor axons</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Trachealis muscle, stimulated by parasympathetic vagal fibers</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Thyroarytenoid muscle, stimulated by external superior laryngeal fibers</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Sternothyroid muscle, stimulated by ansa cervicalis motor branches</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: C</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The posterior gap of the C-shaped tracheal cartilages is bridged by the **trachealis muscle**, composed of transverse smooth muscle bundles embedded in a fibroelastic membrane. Parasympathetic innervation from the **vagus nerve (recurrent laryngeal nerve)** stimulates trachealis contraction. During forced expiration and coughing, active contraction of the trachealis narrows the tracheal lumen by up to 40%, which markedly increases linear airflow velocity (Bernoulli effect), dislodging mucus and foreign particles.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A, B, & D:</em> Vocalis, cricopharyngeus, and thyroarytenoid are striated skeletal muscles of the larynx and pharynx, not the tracheal wall.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Sternothyroid is an infrahyoid strap muscle in the anterior neck.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: RESPIRATORY SYSTEM HISTOLOGY-RES 352.pdf &amp; Theory Screenshot Recall Item 30</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q23: Irritant Receptors (I-Receptors) -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 23</span>
      <span>Category: Neurophysiology of Respiration</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      An industrial worker accidentally inhales concentrated ammonia fumes and sulfur dioxide vapor. Within seconds, he develops intense substernal airway irritation, severe coughing spasms, reflex bronchoconstriction, and rapid shallow tachypneic breathing.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following sensory receptors located within the airway mucosal epithelial lining is primarily responsible for triggering this protective reflex response?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Slowly adapting pulmonary stretch receptors in bronchial smooth muscle</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Rapidly adapting irritant receptors (I-receptors) located between epithelial cells</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Juxtacapillary (J-receptors) embedded within alveolar capillary walls</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Glomus cells situated within the carotid body bifurcations</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Central chemosensitive neurons located on the ventrolateral medulla</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        **Irritant receptors (I-receptors)** are **rapidly adapting sensory nerve endings** located between epithelial cells lining the conducting airways (trachea, bronchi, and large bronchioles). They are stimulated by noxious gases (ammonia, cigarette smoke, SO2), chemical irritants, cold air, and histamine. Sensory afferents travel via **myelinated vagal fibers** to trigger reflex **coughing, sneezing, bronchoconstriction, and hyperpnea/tachypnea**.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> Slowly adapting stretch receptors are located in airway smooth muscle and mediate the Hering-Breuer inflation reflex.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> J-receptors (C-fiber endings) lie adjacent to alveolar capillaries and respond to pulmonary engorgement/edema (causing dyspnea in heart failure).</li>
          <li><em>Option D & E:</em> Carotid and central chemoreceptors monitor blood gases (PaO2, PaCO2, pH), not mechanical or surface chemical airway irritants.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Neural control of respiration.pdf &amp; Theory Screenshot Recall Item 43</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q24: Hering-Breuer Inflation Reflex -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 24</span>
      <span>Category: Neural Regulation of Respiration</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      During mechanical ventilation of an anesthetized 30-year-old patient undergoing abdominal surgery, the ventilator accidentally delivers an excessively large tidal volume of 1,400 mL (normal resting tidal volume ~500 mL). Immediately, spontaneous inspiratory diaphragmatic efforts cease and expiration is prolonged.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following physiological reflexes was activated by this extreme lung distension?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> The Bainbridge reflex stimulating sinus node tachycardia</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex terminating inspiration to prevent overdistension</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> The diving reflex producing profound peripheral vasoconstriction</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> The Bezold-Jarisch reflex inducing profound hypotension and bradycardia</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> The Cushing reflex triggering hypertension and irregular breathing</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        The **Hering-Breuer inflation reflex** is a protective negative-feedback reflex. When the lungs are inflated to large tidal volumes (typically **&gt;1,000 mL to 1,500 mL** in humans, or >3 times normal tidal volume), **slowly adapting pulmonary stretch receptors** embedded within the smooth muscle layer of small airways are activated. These receptors send inhibitory afferent impulses via **myelinated vagus nerve (CN X)** fibers to the dorsal respiratory group (DRG) in the medulla, switching off the inspiratory ramp and preventing mechanical overinflation and alveolar barotrauma.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> The Bainbridge reflex increases heart rate in response to elevated right atrial venous pressure.</li>
          <li><em>Option C:</em> The diving reflex triggers bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction upon facial immersion in cold water.</li>
          <li><em>Option D:</em> The Bezold-Jarisch reflex causes bradycardia and hypotension in response to ventricular chemical or mechanical stimulation.</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> The Cushing reflex is the triad of hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration seen with elevated intracranial pressure.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: Neural control of respiration.pdf &amp; Theory Screenshot Recall Item 44</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q25: Costovertebral Mechanics (Pump-Handle vs. Bucket-Handle) -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 25</span>
      <span>Category: Respiratory Mechanics & Thoracic Kinematics</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A biomechanics researcher uses three-dimensional kinematic motion analysis to evaluate thoracic cage expansion during vigorous inspiration. She observes that elevation of the upper true ribs (ribs 2 through 6) primarily pushes the sternum upward and forward, whereas elevation of the lower false ribs (ribs 7 through 10) causes the lateral curved margins of the ribs to swing outward and upward.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following pairs correctly identifies the biomechanical motion and resulting dimensional increase produced by the movement of **ribs 2 through 6**?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Bucket-handle movement, increasing the lateral transverse diameter of the thoracic cage</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Pump-handle movement, increasing the anteroposterior (AP) diameter of the thoracic cage</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Caliper movement, increasing the vertical dimension of the thoracic cavity</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Piston movement, increasing the intrathoracic subatmospheric negative pressure</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Torsion movement, decreasing the costochondral compliance of the rib cage</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        Thoracic cage expansion during inspiration occurs across distinct anatomical axes:
        1. **Upper ribs (ribs 2 to 6):** Rotate around a transverse axis passing through the costovertebral and costotransverse joints. Elevation pushes the sternum upward and forward like a water pump handle (**"pump-handle movement"**), which primarily **increases the anteroposterior (AP) diameter** of the thorax.
        2. **Lower ribs (ribs 7 to 10):** Rotate around an anteroposterior axis. The lateral middle portions of the ribs swing outward and upward (**"bucket-handle movement"**), which primarily **increases the transverse (lateral) diameter** of the thorax.
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Option A:</em> The bucket-handle movement is performed by lower ribs (7–10) and increases transverse diameter, not the pump-handle motion of ribs 2–6.</li>
          <li><em>Option C & D:</em> Vertical dimension is increased by diaphragmatic contraction (piston-like descent), while caliper movement applies to floating ribs (11–12).</li>
          <li><em>Option E:</em> Torsion is an internal strain, not a kinematic classification of chest wall expansion.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="citation">Source: mechanics of respiration.pdf &amp; Theory Screenshot Recall Item 47</div>
    </div>
  </div>

